│ Event-Aware SAE Trainer — VRAM, Convergence, and Throughput Optimization           │
│                                                                                    │
│ Context                                                                            │
│                                                                                    │
│ Rick wants the trainer to be the fastest, leanest SAE trainer available: minimum   │
│ VRAM, fastest convergence, highest weight quality, well-tuned Lagrangian control,  │
│ correct nudges, a kill-run that fires at the smartest moment, and dead features    │
│ kept low.                                                                          │
│                                                                                    │
│ Two inputs shaped this plan. First, an ultra review on ultra-audit produced ten    │
│ verified findings, seven of them in the stopping path. Second, a direct read of    │
│ sae_trainer_rolling.py (3,752 lines) and sae_scheduler.py (1,597 lines) turned     │
│ up several things the review did not look for, because the review was scoped to    │
│ correctness rather than performance.                                               │
│                                                                                    │
│ The most important finding reframes the VRAM question. Rick's premise is that      │
│ VRAM is dominated by keeping only 2 LLM layers resident. That is true during       │
│ activation capture, but during SAE training the LLM is not resident at all.        │
│ There, VRAM is dominated by [B, F] intermediates: at the default                   │
│ BATCH_TOKENS=32768 and EXPANSION=32 (F = 73,728 on a 2304-dim model), a single     │
│ [B, F] bf16 tensor is 4.8 GB, and the live path holds three of them in             │
│ forward plus roughly four more transients in backward. That is where the headroom  │
│ is, and it is a much bigger lever than the layer window.                           │
│                                                                                    │
│ Second-most important: _JumpReLUWithGate, a custom autograd Function with an       │
│ explicit fused backward, exists in the file and is never called. The live path     │
│ is the hook-based _jumprelu_forward, which allocates a full [B, F] tensor          │
│ purely to attach a grad_fn.                                                        │
│                                                                                    │
│ This plan is ordered by (impact x confidence) / risk, and it is measurement-first. │
│ That ordering is not a stylistic preference: the atlasing census speedup was found │
│ by telemetry showing the GPU at 0% util starved by CPU-side work, not by reading   │
│ code. The same discipline applies here.                                            │
│                                                                                    │
│ Ground rules                                                                       │
│                                                                                    │
│ - All work on a new branch off main. Do not touch ultra-audit or audit-base        │
│   (review scaffolding). Note main is currently ahead of origin by 2 doc commits.   │
│ - Every performance claim needs a before/after number from the same GPU, same      │
│   config, same seed. No claim ships on reasoning alone.                            │
│ - Correctness fixes and performance changes land in separate commits so a          │
│   regression can be bisected to one or the other.                                  │
│ - Do not change control-policy behavior (Lagrangian gains, stop thresholds)        │
│   without an explicit sign-off — those change what the SAE is, not just how        │
│   fast it trains.                                                                  │
