│ Phase 2 — VRAM (the big lever)                                                     │
│                                                                                    │
│ 2a. Wire up the dead fused JumpReLU Function                                       │
│                                                                                    │
│ File: sae_trainer_rolling.py; _JumpReLUWithGate (597-640) is defined and           │
│ never used. jumprelu_with_gate (808) routes to _jumprelu_forward (752).            │
│                                                                                    │
│ The live path builds:                                                              │
│                                                                                    │
│ gate_hard = (pre > threshold).to(pre.dtype)          # [B, F]                      │
│ bridge    = (threshold - threshold.detach()).view(1, -1) * 0.0                     │
│ gate      = gate_hard + bridge                        # [B, F]  <- broadcast alloc │
│ feat_acts = pre * gate                                # [B, F]                     │
│                                                                                    │
│ bridge is a [1, F] zero tensor whose only job is to give gate a grad_fn so         │
│ the registered hooks fire. Broadcasting it costs a full [B, F] allocation —        │
│ 4.8 GB at default config — for graph plumbing. It also relies on mutable instance  │
│ state (self._saved_pre, _saved_grad_feat, _saved_grad_gate), which is              │
│ fragile under gradient accumulation.                                               │
│                                                                                    │
│ _JumpReLUWithGate already does this with explicit save_for_backward and no         │
│ hooks. Switch jumprelu_with_gate to it.                                            │
│                                                                                    │
│ Risk: tests/test_sae_model.py::test_fused_jumprelu_matches_separate currently      │
│ exercises the hook path, so it validates the thing being replaced, not the         │
│ replacement. Extend it to assert both paths agree on feat, gate, grad_pre,         │
│ and grad_log_threshold before switching the default. Keep the hook path behind     │
│ a flag for one release so a regression can be A/B'd.                               │
│                                                                                    │
│ 2b. Recompute gate in backward instead of holding it                               │
│                                                                                    │
│ _JumpReLUWithGate.forward does ctx.save_for_backward(pre, threshold, gate).        │
│ gate is exactly pre > threshold and is trivially recomputed. Dropping it from      │
│ the saved set frees a [B, F] tensor for the whole forward-to-backward window.      │
│                                                                                    │
│ 2c. Chunk the STE threshold reduction over the batch                               │
│                                                                                    │
│ Both the hook path (_log_threshold_hook, 719-751) and the Function's backward      │
│ materialize several [B, F] transients to produce a [F] result:                     │
│                                                                                    │
│ in_band = (pre - threshold).abs() < eps      # 3 intermediates                     │
│ masked  = torch.where(in_band, combined, 0.0)                                      │
│ grad_threshold = -(masked.sum(dim=sum_dims) / (2 * eps))                           │
│                                                                                    │
│ The output is [F]. Chunk over the batch dimension and accumulate, bounding peak    │
│ memory to chunk x F. Reuse the pattern already proven in this file —               │
│ _SparseDecode.backward (549-570) chunks exactly this way with                      │
│ SAE_SPARSE_DECODE_CHUNK. Also do combined.masked_fill_(~in_band, 0.0) in place     │
│ rather than allocating masked, and order the backward so grad_feat is reused       │
│ in place for grad_pre after combined is computed.                                  │
│                                                                                    │
│ 2d. Stop cloning the whole SAE every log step                                      │
│                                                                                    │
│ File: sae_trainer_rolling.py, ~2858-2862 (dead_state_buf) and ~3009                │
│ (best_state_in_memory)                                                             │
│                                                                                    │
│ Both do {k: v.detach().cpu().clone() for k, v in sae.state_dict().items()}. At     │
│ EXPANSION=32 on a 2304-dim model the SAE is ~340 M params — ~1.36 GB per           │
│ clone in fp32. dead_rollback_slots = 4 means ~5.4 GB of host RAM for rollback      │
│ alone, plus another 1.36 GB for best-state, plus an unpinned (therefore blocking)  │
│ D2H copy of that size every LOG_EVERY = 250 steps.                                 │
│                                                                                    │
│ Three fixes, in order of value:                                                    │
│                                                                                    │
│ 1. Only buffer when rollback is plausible. dead_stop_pct = 1.0; if dead is         │
│    far below the ceiling no rollback will ever select that entry. Gate the clone   │
│    on dead > dead_stop_pct * threshold_fraction. In a healthy run this             │
│    eliminates nearly all clones.                                                   │
│ 2. Preallocate pinned host buffers once and copy into them with                    │
│    non_blocking=True, instead of allocating 1.36 GB per log step.                  │
│ 3. Store bf16 in the rollback buffer. It is a fallback checkpoint, not the         │
│    final artifact.                                                                 │
│                                                                                    │
│ 2e. Make the aux (dead-feature) loss sparse                                        │
│                                                                                    │
│ File: sae_trainer_rolling.py, ~2595-2605                                           │
│                                                                                    │
│ pre_dead   = pre[:, dead_indices].relu()          # [B, n_dead]                    │
│ topk_vals, topk_idx = pre_dead.topk(eff_k, dim=-1)                                 │
│ aux_acts   = torch.zeros_like(pre_dead)           # [B, n_dead]                    │
│ aux_acts.scatter_(-1, topk_idx, topk_vals)                                         │
│ W_dec_dead = sae.W_dec.weight.t()[dead_indices]   # [n_dead, d] gather, per microbatch                                                                         │
│ x_aux      = aux_acts @ W_dec_dead                                                 │
│                                                                                    │
│ The aux loss is top-k sparse by construction (AUX_K = 128), but is computed        │
│ densely over all n_dead features. With thousands of dead features this             │
│ allocates three [B, n_dead] tensors and re-gathers the decoder weight every        │
│ microbatch.                                                                        │
│                                                                                    │
│ Replace with an embedding_bag gather-sum over dead_indices[topk_idx] and           │
│ topk_vals — the same primitive _SparseDecode.forward (536-547) already uses.       │
│ That eliminates aux_acts, the dense matmul, and the W_dec_dead gather. Add         │
│ .relu_() in place on the gathered pre_dead.                                        │
│                                                                                    │
│ ---                                                                                │
│                                                                                    │
│ Phase 3 — Throughput                                                               │
│                                                                                    │
│ 3a. Remove per-microbatch CUDA syncs                                               │
│                                                                                    │
│ File: ~2578, ~2616-2617                                                            │
│                                                                                    │
│ - active_max = int(gate_counts.max().item()) (2578) is a hard sync between         │
│   encode and decode, stalling mid-forward. Only used when SAE_SPARSE_DECODE=1      │
│   (default off). Phase 0's fallback-rate telemetry decides whether sparse decode   │
│   earns its place at all; if it does, consider a bounded-estimate scheme with a    │
│   correctness fallback rather than a per-microbatch sync.                          │
│ - accum_recon_loss += recon_loss.item() and accum_l0 += l0_mb.item()               │
│   (2616-2617) sync per microbatch, but the only consumer (current_step_l0 for      │
│   wenc_dampen_factor) runs after the accumulation loop. Accumulate into GPU        │
│   scalars and do one .item() on a stacked 2-element tensor after the loop:         │
│   2 syncs per step instead of 2 x accum_steps.                                     │
│                                                                                    │
│ 3b. Replace the sparse-decode weight-gradient Python loop                          │
│                                                                                    │
│ File: _SparseDecode.backward (549-570)                                             │
│                                                                                    │
│ grad_w_t = torch.zeros_like(weight.t())        # [F, d] alloc every backward       │
│ for s in range(0, B, step):                                                        │
│     contrib = go[s:e].unsqueeze(1) * val[s:e].unsqueeze(2)                         │
│     grad_w_t.index_add_(0, idx[s:e].reshape(-1), ...)                              │
│                                                                                    │
│ At EXPANSION=32 the zeros_like is a ~1 GB allocation on every backward, and        │
│ the Python loop launches several kernels per chunk.                                │
│                                                                                    │
│ The whole loop is one sparse-dense matmul: build a [F, B] sparse COO matrix with   │
│ val at (idx, token) and compute S @ go. Single fused kernel, no Python loop,       │
│ no chunk tuning. Benchmark against the current loop before adopting — and cache    │
│ the grad_w_t buffer across calls either way.                                       │
│                                                                                    │
│ 3c. Halve PCIe traffic in the layer window                                         │
│                                                                                    │
│ File: FloatingLayerWindow (1321-1405)                                              │
│                                                                                    │
│ deactivate() does self.decoder_layers[layer].to("cpu") — a D2H copy of             │
│ weights that never changed. The LLM is frozen during capture, so the CPU copy is   │
│ already authoritative. Each layer visit pays H2D and D2H when only H2D is needed.  │
│                                                                                    │
│ - Keep a persistent (pinned) CPU master copy; on deactivate, release the GPU       │
│   copy without copying back.                                                       │
│ - Pin the master so to(device, non_blocking=True) can actually overlap.            │
│ - Prefetch layer n+1 on a side stream while layer n computes, rather than the      │
│   current serial deactivate-then-activate in set_active (1381-1389).               │
│                                                                                    │
│ This is the one place Rick's "2 layers resident" framing does apply, and the win   │
│ is transfer cost, not residency.                                                   │
│                                                                                    │
