│ 2. A10 — _ev_above_floor_count is incremented on AL convergence, not EV            │
│    (1078). Rename plus a reset-on-transition audit.                                │
│ 3. A6 — pin_timeout_steps drives both a PIN→DESCENT bail (621) and a run           │
│    stop (1093). Make precedence explicit rather than emergent from call order.     │
│ 4. A2 — plateau test is max(recent)/recent[0] - 1.0 (1071-1076), so a              │
│    monotonically declining lambda reads as converged. Switch to a two-sided        │
│    dispersion measure, same knob, same window.                                     │
│ 5. A1 — lambda is clipped to [lambda_l0_min, lambda_l0_max] (761, 1027), so        │
│    a saturated dual is perfectly flat and reads as converged. Treat saturation as  │
│    disqualifying and warn naming the bound hit. Depends on item 4 (same block).    │
│ 6. A8 — best-EV checkpoint can capture weights mid-resample (3007-3010),           │
│    because ev_s is smoothed while state_dict() is instantaneous. Suppress          │
│    capture for a cooldown derived from the smoothing window.                       │
│                                                                                    │
│ Decisions, not patches — write up options and get sign-off, do not implement:      │
│                                                                                    │
│ - A5 / mode exits. _maybe_transition is only called when final_event is            │
│   truthy (710), and every event string in the codebase is a key in                 │
│   event_mode_map, so the "BASELINE" default (1213) is unreachable dead code.       │
│   The only real exit is RECOVERY hitting recovery_max_steps (1201) —               │
│   EXPLORE and STABILIZE are effectively terminal, and explore_lr_factor            │
│   keeps distorting the LR for the rest of the run. Note the re-entry guard (1219)  │
│   covers RECOVERY, STABILIZE and omits EXPLORE.                                    │
│ - B2 / uncoordinated controllers. The dual (_dual_update, 727) and the             │
│   direct threshold nudge (compute_threshold_nudge, 868) both drive L0 during       │
│   DESCENT with nothing coordinating them; they can sum or oppose. PIN freezes      │
│   both, so the problem is confined to DESCENT.                                     │
│                                                                                    │
│ Both bear directly on "is convergence as fast as it can be" and neither should be  │
│ resolved unilaterally.                                                             │
